# Systematic Debugging Skill (Project Scope)
#CHLOEAI:1
TYPE="skill"
MODULE="project_systematic_debugging"
VERSION="Charter-SystematicDebugging-Project-2026-04"
LINEAGE="Adapted from obra/superpowers skills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md"
SCOPE="single project"

# ============================================================
# PURPOSE
# ============================================================
#
# When something is broken, fix the root cause, not the symptom.
# This skill enforces a 4-phase discipline so that debugging produces
# evidence and one targeted fix, instead of a queue of speculative
# patches.

# ============================================================
# WHEN TO INVOKE
# ============================================================
#
# Mandatory triggers:
# - The owner reports a bug or failed live-test
# - Two or more prior fix attempts have failed on the same symptom
# - A behavior change is observed that nobody can immediately explain
#
# Subjective triggers:
# - The owner says "just try X" -- pause and re-frame as a hypothesis
#   to test, not a fix to apply

# ============================================================
# PROTOCOL
# ============================================================
#
# Phase 1: ROOT CAUSE INVESTIGATION
#   Mandatory. Complete this phase BEFORE proposing any fix.
#   Do:
#   - Read the actual error / failure carefully (not a paraphrase)
#   - Reproduce consistently. If you can't reproduce, that IS the
#     finding -- surface it
#   - Check recent changes: git log + AI_HANDOFF.chloeai last 3 entries
#   - Gather evidence: where does the system break? Trace data flow
#     through the involved components
#   Output: a written statement of the form
#     "Symptom X happens when condition Y is true; the failing
#      component is Z; relevant evidence: <list>"
#
# Phase 2: PATTERN ANALYSIS
#   Find a working example of the same pattern and compare.
#   - Read line-by-line. Don't skim
#   - Identify the exact differences between working and broken
#   - Understand the dependencies (what does each side rely on?)
#   Output: a written diff-of-meaning between working and broken,
#           with the suspected delta named explicitly.
#
# Phase 3: HYPOTHESIS
#   Form ONE hypothesis. State it clearly with reasoning.
#   - Design the smallest possible change that tests it
#   - Name what success looks like, name what failure looks like
#   - If uncertainty is high, research first (read source, fetch docs)
#   - DO NOT form parallel hypotheses
#
# Phase 4: IMPLEMENTATION
#   Apply ONE fix that addresses the root cause.
#   - Where possible, write a failing test first (or a live-test
#     scenario that fails before the fix and passes after)
#   - Make the smallest change consistent with the root cause
#   - Verify the fix works (run the test, run the live-test scenario)
#   - Log the result via the experiment format from
#     TEMPLATE_personality.chloeai (change/expect/observe/repro/revert)
#
# Architecture stop:
#   If 3+ fixes have failed against the same symptom, STOP patching.
#   Question the architecture. The bug is upstream of where you've
#   been looking.

# ============================================================
# RULES
# ============================================================
#
# - MUST complete Phase 1 before proposing any fix
# - MUST form one hypothesis at a time
# - MUST identify root cause, not just a path that suppresses symptom
# - MUST NOT bundle multiple speculative changes into one build
# - MUST NOT repeat the "one more fix" pattern past 3 attempts

# ============================================================
# ANTI-PATTERNS
# ============================================================
#
# These look like progress and are not:
# - "Just try changing X and see if it works" -- speculation, not test
# - "Quick patch" -- a patch without root cause is a known-fragile state
# - Listing multiple fixes without investigation -- generates work,
#   not understanding
# - Skipping test creation in favor of "I'll verify manually" -- fine
#   once, not repeatable
# - Adapting a reference pattern differently than the working example
#   without saying why
# - Prioritizing apparent speed over understanding (it is slower)

# ============================================================
# STOP CONDITIONS
# ============================================================
#
# Pause and return control to the owner if:
# - A fix is being proposed before Phase 1 evidence is on paper
# - The hypothesis cannot be stated in one sentence
# - The proposed fix touches a subsystem outside the named symptom
# - 3 fixes have failed (architecture question, not patch question)
# - The symptom can't be reproduced (reproduction is itself the work)

# ============================================================
# WRITEBACK TARGETS
# ============================================================
#
# - The full investigation log goes into AI_HANDOFF.chloeai as a
#   session entry
# - The final fix (or the architecture pivot) gets a DELTA entry
#   per the experiment format
# - If a finding contradicts ai_context/decision_log.md, add a
#   superseding decision entry

# ============================================================
# SELF-TEST
# ============================================================
#
# - Test: Echo module name             --> Expect: "project_systematic_debugging"
# - Test: How many phases?             --> Expect: "four"
# - Test: How many hypotheses at once? --> Expect: "one"
# - Test: After 3 failed fixes?        --> Expect: "stop, question architecture"

